WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.461 --> 00:00:09.940
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:16.660
PRESTON HOLMES: My
name's Preston Holmes.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:19.210
I'm a Solution Architect
on Google Cloud Platform

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:21.500
and I work on projects
with connected devices,

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:26.650
otherwise known as the
Internet of Things, or IoT.

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:29.180
And I want to start with
answering definitively

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:30.560
a question that often comes up.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:34.720
And that is, do we have a
consensus as to what is IoT?

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:36.770
And definitively, I'll
say, no, we do not

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:40.172
have any consensus about
what exactly IoT means.

00:00:40.172 --> 00:00:42.255
And so I'm going to give
you my working definition

00:00:42.255 --> 00:00:43.560
that I find useful for this.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:47.940
And that is that IoT is
really a period of transition

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.030
and transformation.

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:52.370
And that we have everyday
objects, things in our lives,

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:55.590
and they serve some
primary function.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:59.120
And that at some point, we
begin to connect a few of these

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.470
in this sort of awkward
phase where some of them are

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.560
connected to the
network and many aren't.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:07.000
And this is really IoT, the
sort of awkward transition.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.960
And then eventually,
as all of these devices

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.480
become connected by default,
they're no longer really IoT.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:16.805
They just become the things that
we have in our everyday lives.

00:01:16.805 --> 00:01:18.680
And so it's this transition
and more and more

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:20.300
of these things in
our lives becoming

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:25.070
connected by default that
defines what is IoT devices.

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:29.690
Now if that's one working
definition of what is IoT,

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:33.070
then why are we wanting to
connect all these devices?

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:35.400
What is that what's
the point of it all?

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.200
And it's really
about getting access

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.190
to information that wasn't
previously available.

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:42.100
So there's information
in the environment

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.160
all the time, all around us, but
it's not really available to us

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:46.340
as data.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:48.490
So this information
might be something

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:52.710
like the speed of the delivery
vehicle, the utilization

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:54.950
of parking in a city setting.

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:57.320
Might be more information
about the yield

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:00.380
in defect rate of
manufactured products.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:02.350
No matter which vertical
or industry you're in,

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.920
there's probably information
that's valuable to you,

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.919
but you're not getting
any access to today.

00:02:08.919 --> 00:02:12.230
And so this is one goal
for the Internet of Things.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:15.340
To go from information
to insight.

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:17.600
Now, most of the
data that we deal

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.140
with today in a big
data context, is born

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:21.970
is digital data.

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:26.200
Clicks on a website,
application downloads,

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.200
online commerce transactions.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.790
All are born as
digital information.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:32.190
But the kind of
information we're

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:34.690
interested in the Internet
of Things is analog.

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:37.810
And we need a way to capture
that analog information.

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:39.660
And then locate it to
the places where we

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:41.600
can work on a big data tools.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:44.770
So really the Internet of Things
becomes the set of technologies

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:48.192
that are the means to this end.

00:02:48.192 --> 00:02:49.650
And this is really
what I mean here

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:53.810
when I talk about an end-to-end
solution around the IoT.

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:56.704
Now you've likely been hearing
about the Internet of Things

00:02:56.704 --> 00:02:58.370
for some time and the
question comes up,

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:01.850
why hasn't it been
more fully realized?

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:05.750
And there's a number of reasons
why people have been held back.

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:08.220
But first and foremost, I
think is really the complexity

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:10.070
of the domain that's involved.

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:12.580
You've got to
determine and choose

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:14.980
some sort of hardware
device platform.

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:16.370
Got to pick whether
or not to run

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:20.210
an operating system and which
operating system to run.

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:21.970
The networking in the
Internet of Things

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:23.840
is a lot more
varied and diverse.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.250
Some of these networks
are much more spotty,

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:28.790
much more expensive.

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:31.060
There's whole new classes
of networks being built,

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:34.980
specifically for
IoT applications.

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:38.400
You have mobile and cloud
application development.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.070
Each of which are their own
sort of complex domains.

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:43.130
And all of these devices
have the potential

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:44.630
to generate a lot
of data at scale

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:47.587
that might challenge
traditional data infrastructure.

00:03:47.587 --> 00:03:49.420
And then finally, we
all recognize that this

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:51.550
has to be done securely.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:53.840
All eyes are on the
security of IoT.

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:57.050
And the good news is by
paying attention to it early,

00:03:57.050 --> 00:04:00.817
we have the chance to
design security in upfront,

00:04:00.817 --> 00:04:02.900
rather than act reactively,
which is a lot of what

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:05.580
happened with the web.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:09.110
So the technology and
to solve this sort

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:11.480
of end-to-end solution,
I'd like to break down sort

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:13.630
of an architecture for IoT.

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:15.280
And fundamentally
we can recognize

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.510
three primary components.

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:19.550
The device, which is
the physical object

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:22.156
out located in the real world.

00:04:22.156 --> 00:04:23.530
There's often a
gateway, which is

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:27.120
responsible for collecting data
from devices and relaying it.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.640
And then Cloud Platform,
which is increasingly

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.590
sort of the nervous system of
your business or application.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:34.510
Where this IoT data
can be combined

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:37.210
with other non-IoT
data to sort of then

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:40.640
render useful insight
into your applications.

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:44.910
Now if we look at sort of
other pieces of this technology

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:47.150
stack the data
touches throughout,

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:48.910
going from sort
of bottom to top,

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:51.940
you'll recognize a bunch of
sort of familiar Cloud Platform

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:55.290
technology pieces, in terms of
pipeline, storage, analytics,

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:56.750
et cetera.

00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:58.910
And across these
things that touch data,

00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:00.720
we recognize a couple
cross-cutting sort

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.810
of concerns in the areas of
device management and security.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:09.660
And so it's often unclear how
our Cloud pieces necessarily

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:11.765
fit into this end-to-end stack.

00:05:11.765 --> 00:05:13.640
So a lot of what we'll
be talking about today

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:17.680
is some of the devices
and gateway pieces.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:19.960
So what this map or
framework, we can break down

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:22.060
some of the technical
considerations

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:24.340
and we'll kind of use
this throughout the talk.

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:26.710
Starting with devices.

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:29.557
So as I said, devices
are sort of the thing

00:05:29.557 --> 00:05:30.640
of the Internet of Things.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:32.290
It's thing out in the world.

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:34.620
But a thing might be
as small and simple

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:38.010
as a tiny sensor located
inside the door hinge.

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:41.990
Or a thing might be as
gigantic as an entire oil rig.

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:45.020
And these devices might
be compound and mapped

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:48.630
in sort of a hierarchy,
where you might have a ship

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:50.760
and on that ship is a
pump and both of those

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:52.551
are related to each
other, both are things.

00:05:52.551 --> 00:05:54.820
And how those are graphed
is going to really depend

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:56.700
on your specific domain model.

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:58.890
Your domain specialties.

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:01.160
But at the end of
the day, a device

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:05.320
is a physical thing out
in the physical world.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:08.220
And now, a device is really
just a tiny computer,

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:10.730
so it's both
familiar and foreign.

00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:12.980
You can recognize all
the familiar components

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:15.970
of a computer inside
any IoT device.

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:18.760
Fundamentally, it's composed
of hardware and software.

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:21.600
And it is different
than the computers

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:22.520
of our everyday lives.

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:25.300
Fundamentally, the
inputs and outputs

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:28.170
are going to be very different
and a lot more sort of exotic

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:29.670
than the familiar
mouse and keyboard

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:31.970
and monitor that you
have on your computer.

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:34.680
For sensor inputs, there's
a wide variety of sensors

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:37.700
available for gases,
infrared, et cetera.

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:41.782
But an input might be as humble
and simple as a button located

00:06:41.782 --> 00:06:44.240
in just the right place at just
the right time for somebody

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:47.820
to signal some
event has occurred.

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:51.340
Now not all devices are
going to have outputs.

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:53.360
But outputs can range, as well.

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:56.000
So you may have something
as simple as a buzzer.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:59.260
Simply let somebody know
something's happening.

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:01.445
An output can be a
gigantic hydraulic lift

00:07:01.445 --> 00:07:03.180
that can lift several tons.

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:05.175
So again, these things
are what really define

00:07:05.175 --> 00:07:06.790
IoT devices as different.

00:07:06.790 --> 00:07:10.160
Now communications, or
CALMS, is really technically

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:12.310
just another form
of input and output.

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:16.540
I do call it out separately
because the diversity of radios

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:18.080
and radio types
in communications

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:21.320
available for IoT
of devices, makes

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:23.610
communication somewhat
of a special case

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:26.150
for Internet of Things.

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:27.760
Now obviously, on
top of this you

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:32.420
run bootloaders, which run
and load an operating system,

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:32.920
potentially.

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:35.211
And then all of your libraries
and application software

00:07:35.211 --> 00:07:38.160
then runs on this device.

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:43.200
Now like I said, there's this
real diversity and explosion

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:44.724
of hardware platforms.

00:07:44.724 --> 00:07:46.390
It seems like almost
every week, there's

00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:49.600
new types of boards coming
out, new technology.

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:52.090
But really at the end of the
day, as a software developer

00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:55.050
you probably think of the device
in terms of the information

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:56.970
which it provides.

00:07:56.970 --> 00:07:58.960
How do I model and
manage information

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:00.600
this device is working with?

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:03.000
And we can recognize sort
of the four primary kinds

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:04.500
of information.

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:05.870
The first is metadata.

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:08.790
So this is really
information about the device.

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:11.260
This is things like
a device identifier.

00:08:11.260 --> 00:08:14.560
It might include a firmware
version, location date,

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:17.220
installation location
and date, et cetera.

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:18.470
Second is state.

00:08:18.470 --> 00:08:20.610
This is sort of information
about the condition

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:21.570
of the device.

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:24.040
Not necessarily the
condition of the environment,

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:26.410
but it's a current
condition of the device.

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:29.440
Might be read only,
or read-write.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:32.650
It is going to be
something that is along

00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:34.919
the lines of like the set
point for a thermostat,

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:37.601
or some sample
rate for frequency.

00:08:37.601 --> 00:08:39.059
And so this is not
something you're

00:08:39.059 --> 00:08:40.880
going to be changing
and resetting

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:44.410
at very high frequencies.

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:47.410
Commands are type
of information which

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:50.160
indicate some action to
be taken by a device.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:52.300
So this is usually done
is some sort of a handler

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:55.530
or remote procedure call
that's running on the device.

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:58.820
And these actions are
often not idempotent,

00:08:58.820 --> 00:09:02.580
which means that if you send
the same command multiple times,

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:05.384
you might not be getting simply
the same outcome every time.

00:09:05.384 --> 00:09:07.300
And you can imagine if
you have a command that

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:10.629
says Run Self Cleaning
Cycle, and you do

00:09:10.629 --> 00:09:11.920
that once and now you're clean.

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:14.290
If you keep running
it multiple times,

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:17.180
it's not necessarily
going change the result

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:20.830
the same way it changed at
the first time, unlike state.

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:22.640
And then finally, telemetry.

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:25.350
Now telemetry is really
the eyes and ears

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:26.980
of the Internet of Things.

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:29.600
It's sort of the
source a lot of what we

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:31.500
have as the environmental data.

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:33.190
And this telemetry
can take advantage

00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:36.000
of that diversity of sensor
types I mentioned earlier.

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:41.800
Now, if you're going to bother
to sort of define the device,

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:45.420
pick hardware platforms, and
model the information they

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:47.926
produce or consume,
the odds are you're

00:09:47.926 --> 00:09:49.800
going to have more than
one device out there.

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:51.927
And as soon as you have
more than one device,

00:09:51.927 --> 00:09:54.510
you need to consider how you're
going to do device management.

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:56.930
And device management
broadly addresses the tasks

00:09:56.930 --> 00:10:00.380
required to make these
devices functional and useful

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:02.410
in your project.

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:04.450
And this involves
pieces of functionality,

00:10:04.450 --> 00:10:09.290
but run both on the device
as well as in the cloud.

00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:11.990
Now at Cloud
Platform, we're pretty

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:14.050
versatile in terms of
how we work with you

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:16.069
to do device management.

00:10:16.069 --> 00:10:17.860
We have integrations
with Brillo and Weave,

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:19.730
which you'll be hearing
more about later,

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:21.680
that handles device management.

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:25.800
We work with a number of device
management platform partners

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:27.860
who take care of all
the Edge communications

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:29.320
and set up of provisioning.

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:31.684
And there are number
of open-source and sort

00:10:31.684 --> 00:10:32.850
of do-it-yourself solutions.

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:37.180
But no matter which solution you
choose for device management,

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:40.320
we can recognize kind of the
key functions that are achieved.

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:43.740
First is, you need to provision
a device on to the network.

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:45.990
It needs to be able to connect
to the local networking

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:48.760
environment where
that device sits.

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:51.310
You need to register this
device in some sort of registry

00:10:51.310 --> 00:10:53.400
so you can keep track
of which devices you

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:54.910
have under management.

00:10:54.910 --> 00:10:56.930
You need to authorize a device.

00:10:56.930 --> 00:11:00.200
You need to make sure that
you're only speaking to devices

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:02.584
that are part of your fleet.

00:11:02.584 --> 00:11:04.500
And you need to make
sure the devices can only

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:07.990
reach the backend systems
they're supposed to.

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:12.090
You need to monitor and
perform operational management

00:11:12.090 --> 00:11:14.070
across the fleet or
groups of devices

00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:16.620
so that you know that
collectively and aggregate

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:20.070
your entire fleet is
performing as you expect it to.

00:11:20.070 --> 00:11:22.830
And finally, you need to be
able to ensure that devices

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:24.000
are being up-to-date.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:27.020
Now this is not only
important for making

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:29.820
sure the device is gaining
new functions over time

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:31.520
and new features,
but it's critically

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:33.259
important for security.

00:11:33.259 --> 00:11:35.300
That it's always being
maintained with the latest

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:36.055
security updates.

00:11:38.397 --> 00:11:40.480
So let's talk about a
couple of customer examples.

00:11:43.236 --> 00:11:44.610
I want to tell
you about how Nest

00:11:44.610 --> 00:11:46.270
has been able to
simplify the deployment

00:11:46.270 --> 00:11:48.603
of connected cameras, as part
of their ongoing migration

00:11:48.603 --> 00:11:52.100
from another cloud platform.

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:54.790
So today, in the completely
regionalized deployment,

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:57.770
cameras first must contacted
a dispatcher in one region

00:11:57.770 --> 00:12:00.540
before connecting to a home
server in another region.

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:03.310
And then that data is
stored, per region.

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:05.030
And when it's stored
in a single region,

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:07.460
it gets copied to other
regions for durability

00:12:07.460 --> 00:12:08.630
and availability.

00:12:08.630 --> 00:12:10.110
Now, in contrast,
the cameras that

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:12.550
are now connecting to
Google Cloud Platform,

00:12:12.550 --> 00:12:15.490
use our Global Load Balancing
service to immediately reached

00:12:15.490 --> 00:12:19.210
the regional server that they
ultimately stay connected to.

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:21.620
And the data from
that server is stored

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:24.250
in services with
global storage scope,

00:12:24.250 --> 00:12:27.130
so they avoid the complexity
of needing to synchronize data

00:12:27.130 --> 00:12:28.322
across regions.

00:12:28.322 --> 00:12:30.030
And get that sort of
global availability.

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:37.565
Making objects easy and pleasant
to use and easy to orchestrate

00:12:37.565 --> 00:12:39.860
is something Philips Lighting
has done successfully

00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:41.940
with the HUE color
changing light bulb.

00:12:41.940 --> 00:12:44.550
It's one or more familiar
and sort of widely

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:48.100
deployed IoT devices
out in the world.

00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:49.960
Many of you may have one.

00:12:49.960 --> 00:12:52.840
Now Philips, with the help
of one of our partners, Q42,

00:12:52.840 --> 00:12:55.210
was able to build and
scale the infrastructure

00:12:55.210 --> 00:12:59.040
to support the HUE application
and API on Cloud Platform.

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:01.630
This low latency
and reliable API

00:13:01.630 --> 00:13:04.790
is part of the HUE experience.

00:13:04.790 --> 00:13:08.610
Philips and HUE,
Philips and Q42,

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:11.120
were able to take advantage
of some features in Container

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:13.500
Engine to allow the
backend to this system

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:16.120
to be steadily improved
and steadily released

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:17.630
by taking advantage
of the features

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:21.360
such as rolling updates
on Container Engine.

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:23.430
They're also able to
launch new and interesting

00:13:23.430 --> 00:13:26.200
small experiments
very, very quickly

00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:28.580
on their existing
Container Engine cluster

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:30.620
and at almost no cost,
no additional cost,

00:13:30.620 --> 00:13:32.920
to run those experiments
side by side.

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:35.510
And all of that Container
Engine infrastructure

00:13:35.510 --> 00:13:37.670
is then able to
connect to our data

00:13:37.670 --> 00:13:40.060
and analytics and
monitoring platforms

00:13:40.060 --> 00:13:43.100
elsewhere on Cloud Platform.

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:47.110
So the light bulb is a great
example of a device which

00:13:47.110 --> 00:13:49.805
by itself cannot
reach Cloud Platform.

00:13:49.805 --> 00:13:52.530
And I'd mentioned
gateways in the beginning.

00:13:52.530 --> 00:13:56.390
And all HUE light bulb shift
with sort of a gateway or hub.

00:13:56.390 --> 00:14:01.160
And I said this is often a key
component in IoT architectures.

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:04.690
And I want to explain a little
bit more about why that's so.

00:14:04.690 --> 00:14:07.920
And it comes down to there's
a whole class of devices

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:11.170
out there that are not able
to reach the Cloud for one

00:14:11.170 --> 00:14:12.390
reason or another.

00:14:12.390 --> 00:14:14.964
This might be that they do not
have any radical connectivity

00:14:14.964 --> 00:14:15.630
to the internet.

00:14:15.630 --> 00:14:20.540
For example, Bluetooth is not
directly routable to TCP/IP.

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:23.260
It might be that they don't
have the processing capability

00:14:23.260 --> 00:14:25.960
to perform the secured
HTTPS transport

00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:30.290
protocol that all of our
Cloud Platform APIs require.

00:14:30.290 --> 00:14:34.430
It also might be that they don't
have the power budget in order

00:14:34.430 --> 00:14:35.780
to support the networking.

00:14:35.780 --> 00:14:38.100
They may be very low power.

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:41.580
And finally, there are
perspectives in a setting that

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:44.730
require a device to look at
multiple devices in that area

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:46.150
and do some pre-computing.

00:14:46.150 --> 00:14:50.020
So any one device isn't going
to give you that local aggregate

00:14:50.020 --> 00:14:52.050
and pre-processing setting.

00:14:52.050 --> 00:14:55.490
And that's part of
what a gateway can do.

00:14:55.490 --> 00:14:58.210
So we have a demo outside
running with Intel.

00:14:58.210 --> 00:15:00.920
One of our partners in the
gateway space is Intel.

00:15:00.920 --> 00:15:04.020
They make an industrial
IoT gateway platform.

00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:06.780
And we've worked with them on an
integration with Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:15:06.780 --> 00:15:09.580
And the scenario we
worked on is this idea

00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:11.950
of tracking the
environmental conditions

00:15:11.950 --> 00:15:14.570
and locations of pallet goods.

00:15:14.570 --> 00:15:17.060
So this starts with an
Eddystone Bluetooth Beacon.

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:20.740
Now, Eddystone is an open source
Bluetooth spec from Google

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:24.230
to find a couple data types that
are broadcast over Bluetooth,

00:15:24.230 --> 00:15:27.970
including an ID, as well as
telemetry frames for things

00:15:27.970 --> 00:15:30.800
like temperature or humidity.

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:34.026
And the gateway collects these
from the Bluetooth Beacon

00:15:34.026 --> 00:15:35.900
and then sends the
information up to Pub/Sub.

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:40.920
On the gateway is also
a developer experience,

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:43.050
a sort of a developer
UI, that you can easily

00:15:43.050 --> 00:15:45.510
connect various sensors
directly to the gateway.

00:15:45.510 --> 00:15:47.850
And then these automatically
gain from this integration

00:15:47.850 --> 00:15:49.470
to Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:15:49.470 --> 00:15:52.910
The gateway also runs a
set of libraries for doing

00:15:52.910 --> 00:15:55.010
runtime Edge processing.

00:15:55.010 --> 00:15:57.690
And this is useful when
you have networking

00:15:57.690 --> 00:16:00.580
where you want to be
somewhat miserly about what

00:16:00.580 --> 00:16:03.570
you're actually transmitting
on the network to the cloud.

00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:06.000
So if you can do some
degree of Edge processing,

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:10.340
you can really send up the
right quantity and kind

00:16:10.340 --> 00:16:13.760
of condensed version
of information.

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:17.340
And so this idea of connectivity
being a specific challenge

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:21.040
in IoT solutions comes
up because the location

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:23.280
where this information
you're interested in

00:16:23.280 --> 00:16:25.390
is going to be
potentially in who

00:16:25.390 --> 00:16:26.980
knows what kind of environment.

00:16:26.980 --> 00:16:29.870
And the world is not
blanketed with Wi-Fi.

00:16:29.870 --> 00:16:32.810
Wi-Fi is limited to
sort of the places

00:16:32.810 --> 00:16:35.320
where people are today,
for the most part.

00:16:35.320 --> 00:16:37.200
And so one of the
ways we're looking

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:40.370
at helping extend
connectivity to Cloud

00:16:40.370 --> 00:16:43.480
Platform through partners,
is with a project

00:16:43.480 --> 00:16:46.700
we're doing with Particle
I/O. Now Particle

00:16:46.700 --> 00:16:49.310
is one of the more widely used
Internet of Things platforms.

00:16:49.310 --> 00:16:51.650
They've got over 60,000
developers on their platform

00:16:51.650 --> 00:16:52.740
already.

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:55.490
And most recently, they
released the Electron.

00:16:55.490 --> 00:16:57.300
The Electron is a
small powerful IoT

00:16:57.300 --> 00:17:00.630
device with built in
cellular connectivity.

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:02.910
And so this gives
you the ability

00:17:02.910 --> 00:17:05.300
to have this device
connect to the Cloud

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:07.025
in over 100 countries.

00:17:07.025 --> 00:17:08.400
And we've partnered
with Particle

00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:09.858
to make it easier
than ever to pipe

00:17:09.858 --> 00:17:11.980
data from any connected
Particle device

00:17:11.980 --> 00:17:15.260
directly into Google
Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:17:15.260 --> 00:17:17.400
Now anyone who's
visited Google may

00:17:17.400 --> 00:17:20.430
have seen these colorful bikes
that we have all around campus

00:17:20.430 --> 00:17:21.670
called G Bikes.

00:17:21.670 --> 00:17:24.290
And as Google's
campus has grown,

00:17:24.290 --> 00:17:26.450
the G Bike has become
the sort of key way

00:17:26.450 --> 00:17:29.370
that people get from building
to building throughout the day.

00:17:29.370 --> 00:17:32.610
But the team that operates
G Bikes for Google

00:17:32.610 --> 00:17:35.219
has very little visibility
into where these bikes tend

00:17:35.219 --> 00:17:37.260
to go during the day, and
how they tend to clump,

00:17:37.260 --> 00:17:38.780
and where they are.

00:17:38.780 --> 00:17:40.920
So working with
Particle, we've sort

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:43.850
of attach some of these
Particle Electrons to bikes.

00:17:43.850 --> 00:17:46.120
And we're able to collect
the locations of how

00:17:46.120 --> 00:17:48.560
they move throughout
campus during the day.

00:17:48.560 --> 00:17:50.600
We stream this through
Pub/Sub into BigQuery

00:17:50.600 --> 00:17:52.300
and then we can run
queries that allow

00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:57.270
us to plot their locations with
heat map APIs in Google Maps.

00:17:57.270 --> 00:17:59.370
This is really invaluable
for that G Bike team

00:17:59.370 --> 00:18:03.670
to understand what the usage
is and where these bikes are

00:18:03.670 --> 00:18:05.460
throughout the day.

00:18:05.460 --> 00:18:08.680
So connectivity involves
things like networks,

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:10.930
but it also involves
things like protocols.

00:18:10.930 --> 00:18:14.920
Yesterday we announced support--
Alpha support-- for gRPC

00:18:14.920 --> 00:18:16.140
for Pub/Sub.

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:20.000
And gRPC is an open-source
binary protocol

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:22.850
that we'll be supporting
across many of our products.

00:18:22.850 --> 00:18:25.590
But in IoT, there
are other protocols

00:18:25.590 --> 00:18:27.420
that are very commonly found.

00:18:27.420 --> 00:18:30.000
And one of those is MQDT.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:32.770
So a partner of
ours, Agosto, has

00:18:32.770 --> 00:18:36.910
been working on building an
MQTT solution on Compute Engine.

00:18:36.910 --> 00:18:41.580
MQTT, as a protocol,
is well-suited for IoT.

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:45.080
It has a very low footprint,
both in terms of network usage,

00:18:45.080 --> 00:18:47.680
but also is available
as libraries

00:18:47.680 --> 00:18:51.300
for many small
microcontroller devices today.

00:18:51.300 --> 00:18:56.475
And so what Agosto has done,
is build a custom MQTT broker

00:18:56.475 --> 00:18:59.020
that is based on RabbitMQ.

00:18:59.020 --> 00:19:03.690
And it allows us to focus the
protocol translation from MQTT

00:19:03.690 --> 00:19:07.420
to Pub/Sub and lets Pub/Sub,
as a managed service,

00:19:07.420 --> 00:19:09.970
handle all the
durability and managed

00:19:09.970 --> 00:19:11.870
scale of storage of events.

00:19:11.870 --> 00:19:13.990
So the Compute
Engine, we can focus

00:19:13.990 --> 00:19:16.830
on using the networking
performance and CPU

00:19:16.830 --> 00:19:18.770
performance, but not
necessarily worry

00:19:18.770 --> 00:19:22.040
about storing all of the events.

00:19:22.040 --> 00:19:25.520
And this solution
allows you to scale

00:19:25.520 --> 00:19:28.600
this out from pilot
to production,

00:19:28.600 --> 00:19:31.410
very cost effectively.

00:19:31.410 --> 00:19:34.020
Now what I've been
talking about today

00:19:34.020 --> 00:19:36.640
is how things get solved today.

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:40.370
This domain of complexity
is all solvable,

00:19:40.370 --> 00:19:42.270
but it can get
definitely better.

00:19:42.270 --> 00:19:44.740
And at Google, we're
working on making it better

00:19:44.740 --> 00:19:46.540
across the experience.

00:19:46.540 --> 00:19:50.690
And it's been part of our DNA to
work with devices from Android

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:54.173
phones, to Chromecast,
and Chromebooks.

00:19:54.173 --> 00:19:56.423
So I invite Jeff Chen, the
Product Manager from Brillo

00:19:56.423 --> 00:19:58.800
and Weave to tell you more
about the Brillo and Weave

00:19:58.800 --> 00:20:01.435
project at Brillo and how we're
looking to improve devices.

00:20:01.435 --> 00:20:02.685
JEFF CHEN: Thank you, Preston.

00:20:02.685 --> 00:20:05.060
[APPLAUSE]

00:20:05.060 --> 00:20:06.420
Good afternoon.

00:20:06.420 --> 00:20:07.757
So my name is Jeff Chen.

00:20:07.757 --> 00:20:10.340
I'm one of the product managers
on the Google Brillo and Weave

00:20:10.340 --> 00:20:13.100
teams, so I'm a device
guy, the hardware guy.

00:20:13.100 --> 00:20:15.460
And so I wanted to
first introduce to you

00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:17.370
what Brillo and Weave are.

00:20:17.370 --> 00:20:21.401
This is Google's offering,
specifically in the IoT space.

00:20:21.401 --> 00:20:23.650
Well when we sought out to
build out Brillo and Weave,

00:20:23.650 --> 00:20:27.365
we wanted to address
three major objectives.

00:20:27.365 --> 00:20:28.990
The first one is that
we wanted to help

00:20:28.990 --> 00:20:31.980
device makers a build for IoT.

00:20:31.980 --> 00:20:34.480
So at Google, we have been
building connected devices.

00:20:34.480 --> 00:20:37.250
Android, Chromebooks,
Chromecast, and so on.

00:20:37.250 --> 00:20:38.960
And we realized
that these solutions

00:20:38.960 --> 00:20:42.770
could be benefited from third
party device manufacturers

00:20:42.770 --> 00:20:45.460
because they struggle
with trying to figure out

00:20:45.460 --> 00:20:48.430
how to make devices
cost effectively,

00:20:48.430 --> 00:20:50.470
or be able to build
with security.

00:20:50.470 --> 00:20:53.900
So we wanted to help device
makers prototype their device

00:20:53.900 --> 00:20:56.590
applications quickly and
then, more importantly,

00:20:56.590 --> 00:20:59.390
port that over to the
production hardware

00:20:59.390 --> 00:21:01.930
without requiring major surgery.

00:21:01.930 --> 00:21:03.040
And it doesn't end there.

00:21:03.040 --> 00:21:05.771
With connected devices,
it's really important

00:21:05.771 --> 00:21:07.520
to be in a position
where you can actually

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:09.980
monitor the devices
that are in the field

00:21:09.980 --> 00:21:12.510
and be able to push
out updates to them

00:21:12.510 --> 00:21:15.460
to resolve any security
concerns or to update

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:19.030
new features to continue to
improve the user experience.

00:21:19.030 --> 00:21:22.760
Secondly, we wanted to
create an open ecosystem.

00:21:22.760 --> 00:21:25.570
As we observe the growth
of these IoT ecosystems,

00:21:25.570 --> 00:21:29.660
we saw that there were
subnets of siloed ecosystems

00:21:29.660 --> 00:21:32.640
that were causing more
and more fragmentation.

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:37.044
And while there were some
very good reasons for vendors

00:21:37.044 --> 00:21:38.960
to lock down the
connectivity of these devices

00:21:38.960 --> 00:21:41.740
with proprietary
protocols, it keeps us

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:46.122
from getting to this truly
open Internet of Things.

00:21:46.122 --> 00:21:47.580
And so the only
way that we can get

00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:49.800
into this Internet of
Things is by embracing

00:21:49.800 --> 00:21:53.510
open and standardized
communication protocols.

00:21:53.510 --> 00:21:56.920
Which leads us to the third and
final objective that we had.

00:21:56.920 --> 00:22:01.030
And that is that we can
create an ecosystem of devices

00:22:01.030 --> 00:22:03.410
with open protocols
so that we can create

00:22:03.410 --> 00:22:06.580
a rich ecosystem of
apps and services

00:22:06.580 --> 00:22:08.010
that could be transformative.

00:22:08.010 --> 00:22:09.800
So think about the web.

00:22:09.800 --> 00:22:12.390
Think about mobile Android.

00:22:12.390 --> 00:22:16.200
Now, the intelligence and
the smarts of these devices

00:22:16.200 --> 00:22:18.930
will not come from a single
vendor, nor from Google.

00:22:18.930 --> 00:22:21.110
It comes from you.

00:22:21.110 --> 00:22:22.330
Developers.

00:22:22.330 --> 00:22:25.820
Developers that want to build
applications and services that

00:22:25.820 --> 00:22:29.580
can communicate across
devices, from different brands.

00:22:29.580 --> 00:22:32.570
And that can build rich
analytics around these devices

00:22:32.570 --> 00:22:34.760
to help make these
devices better

00:22:34.760 --> 00:22:36.950
and more useful to the user.

00:22:36.950 --> 00:22:40.050
So that's why we built
Brillo and Weave.

00:22:40.050 --> 00:22:42.730
Because ultimately
we see that the magic

00:22:42.730 --> 00:22:46.250
comes from a distributed
world of devices.

00:22:46.250 --> 00:22:49.490
And we think that the
power of this new internet,

00:22:49.490 --> 00:22:54.780
will require the ecosystem to
build devices cost effectively

00:22:54.780 --> 00:22:56.130
and at scale.

00:22:56.130 --> 00:22:58.470
These devices will have to
communicate with each other

00:22:58.470 --> 00:22:59.470
through open protocols.

00:22:59.470 --> 00:23:02.820
Where the smarts
are in the device,

00:23:02.820 --> 00:23:05.720
on clients, or in the Cloud.

00:23:05.720 --> 00:23:08.650
So let me tell you
about what Brillo is.

00:23:08.650 --> 00:23:10.410
Well, Brillo is an
operating system

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:13.500
that's maintained by Google.

00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:16.650
It is a version of Android.

00:23:16.650 --> 00:23:20.100
And it is supported by a
variety of silicon vendors.

00:23:20.100 --> 00:23:23.450
And it has proven scale to
reach over one billion Android

00:23:23.450 --> 00:23:24.930
devices, right?

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:30.460
So we recognize that deploying
a full-blown stack of Android

00:23:30.460 --> 00:23:32.960
is a little bit overkill
for connected devices.

00:23:32.960 --> 00:23:34.890
So we built a
stripped-down version

00:23:34.890 --> 00:23:38.890
that was targeted specifically
for connected devices.

00:23:38.890 --> 00:23:41.030
So device manufacturers
can leverage

00:23:41.030 --> 00:23:44.300
all of the benefits of the
Android hardware ecosystem.

00:23:44.300 --> 00:23:47.000
So if a chip vendor
supports Android,

00:23:47.000 --> 00:23:50.120
it can essentially
support Brillo.

00:23:50.120 --> 00:23:52.960
Now the second aspect of
Brillo is about security.

00:23:52.960 --> 00:23:56.990
And Brillo enables
devices to be secure.

00:23:56.990 --> 00:23:59.500
Because fundamentally,
we believe

00:23:59.500 --> 00:24:02.960
that security has to be
built into the device for IoT

00:24:02.960 --> 00:24:03.590
technology.

00:24:03.590 --> 00:24:06.006
We believe that, not only do
you need to have it built in,

00:24:06.006 --> 00:24:09.340
but you also need a way to be
able to fix issues that are

00:24:09.340 --> 00:24:12.130
in the field as they come up.

00:24:12.130 --> 00:24:14.450
So we've built a number
of security features

00:24:14.450 --> 00:24:16.780
into Brillo that will
address these problems.

00:24:16.780 --> 00:24:18.640
Including verified
boot, which means

00:24:18.640 --> 00:24:20.440
that the device
will run code that

00:24:20.440 --> 00:24:23.500
is trusted from
the manufacturer.

00:24:23.500 --> 00:24:27.140
We also make sure that access
to the device and all the data

00:24:27.140 --> 00:24:29.900
that's produced
through these devices

00:24:29.900 --> 00:24:32.850
is controlled by the user.

00:24:32.850 --> 00:24:35.620
And that any data that is
actually stored on the device

00:24:35.620 --> 00:24:39.810
or transmitted across the
communication pathways

00:24:39.810 --> 00:24:42.600
are all encrypted and secure.

00:24:42.600 --> 00:24:45.690
And then lastly with Brillo,
it comes with services

00:24:45.690 --> 00:24:47.790
like analytics and updates.

00:24:47.790 --> 00:24:49.660
So for example,
device manufacturers

00:24:49.660 --> 00:24:51.580
can get a readout
of all the features

00:24:51.580 --> 00:24:54.690
and the usage of their device
population in aggregate,

00:24:54.690 --> 00:24:57.410
and figure out how the users
are using their devices.

00:24:57.410 --> 00:25:01.240
But also, we provide an
infrastructure to push updates.

00:25:01.240 --> 00:25:03.720
So that as security
threats come up

00:25:03.720 --> 00:25:06.154
or you see new features
that you want to deploy,

00:25:06.154 --> 00:25:08.070
that you can have a
pathway to be able to push

00:25:08.070 --> 00:25:11.250
those updates to those devices.

00:25:11.250 --> 00:25:12.140
Now, for Weave.

00:25:12.140 --> 00:25:13.400
What is Weave?

00:25:13.400 --> 00:25:16.660
Weave is our open
communications platform.

00:25:16.660 --> 00:25:18.830
It consists of three components.

00:25:18.830 --> 00:25:22.550
A device library, client
API, and a web server--

00:25:22.550 --> 00:25:23.850
Cloud server.

00:25:23.850 --> 00:25:26.240
If you build Weave
into your device,

00:25:26.240 --> 00:25:29.280
it's easy for third party
application developers

00:25:29.280 --> 00:25:33.060
to access your Weave devices
from their phone, or a cloud

00:25:33.060 --> 00:25:37.070
service through Weave
mobile and Cloud APIs.

00:25:37.070 --> 00:25:39.910
We believe in
interoperability so much

00:25:39.910 --> 00:25:43.900
that we built out device schemas
for a specific set of device

00:25:43.900 --> 00:25:44.950
categories.

00:25:44.950 --> 00:25:46.940
So that third party
app developers

00:25:46.940 --> 00:25:49.810
could use these schemas to
build apps to monitor the device

00:25:49.810 --> 00:25:53.980
state, to control them, and
to build analytics and richer

00:25:53.980 --> 00:25:56.980
experiences across
all of these devices.

00:25:56.980 --> 00:26:00.530
And in addition to
offering third party APIs,

00:26:00.530 --> 00:26:03.490
we also offer a
seamless integration

00:26:03.490 --> 00:26:07.640
into Google services, such as
the Google Voice assistance.

00:26:10.200 --> 00:26:13.380
So with Brillo
and Weave devices,

00:26:13.380 --> 00:26:16.850
this platform enables
an ease of use and ease

00:26:16.850 --> 00:26:20.460
of building devices and creating
a standardized device data.

00:26:20.460 --> 00:26:24.950
And we expect these
devices to generate data.

00:26:24.950 --> 00:26:26.850
And a lot of it.

00:26:26.850 --> 00:26:29.000
So Google Cloud
Platform for IoT data

00:26:29.000 --> 00:26:32.160
offers a proven
end-to-end solution

00:26:32.160 --> 00:26:35.070
to make sense of
large amounts of data

00:26:35.070 --> 00:26:37.230
and produce meaningful insights.

00:26:37.230 --> 00:26:40.600
These insights can help
manufacturers determine

00:26:40.600 --> 00:26:43.520
what features to add or
remove on their product,

00:26:43.520 --> 00:26:47.770
help app makers develop novel
new experiences and services,

00:26:47.770 --> 00:26:51.240
and help enterprises manage
their fleet of devices

00:26:51.240 --> 00:26:54.280
for optimal performance
and security.

00:26:54.280 --> 00:26:57.120
So that gives you a little
bit of an overview what Google

00:26:57.120 --> 00:27:00.910
is doing in the device
ecosystem to enable an operating

00:27:00.910 --> 00:27:03.620
system, Brillo, and a
communications platform called

00:27:03.620 --> 00:27:04.350
Weave.

00:27:04.350 --> 00:27:07.410
So our team looks forward to all
the amazing and awesome things

00:27:07.410 --> 00:27:09.950
that you guys are going to
be doing on the Cloud side

00:27:09.950 --> 00:27:12.957
to take advantage of these
smart and connected devices.

00:27:12.957 --> 00:27:13.540
Thank you all.

00:27:13.540 --> 00:27:14.748
I'll hand it back to Preston.

00:27:14.748 --> 00:27:16.512
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:18.464 --> 00:27:19.928
PRESTON HOLMES: Thanks, Jeff.

00:27:24.320 --> 00:27:26.920
So as I said earlier, much of
what we've talked about today

00:27:26.920 --> 00:27:29.540
has been about the technology
of the Internet of Things

00:27:29.540 --> 00:27:33.660
that runs outside of the
context of Cloud Platform.

00:27:33.660 --> 00:27:36.120
Now this is important
because, unless you clearly

00:27:36.120 --> 00:27:40.290
understand how the
on-ramps for IoT data work

00:27:40.290 --> 00:27:42.260
and how to solve
for them, you can't

00:27:42.260 --> 00:27:44.350
take advantage of all
the great big data

00:27:44.350 --> 00:27:45.970
tools we have on Cloud Platform.

00:27:45.970 --> 00:27:47.820
So it's important to
understand how to get

00:27:47.820 --> 00:27:50.540
the data on to the Cloud.

00:27:50.540 --> 00:27:52.290
But without a doubt,
data and analytics

00:27:52.290 --> 00:27:54.480
is our strong suit
on Cloud Platform.

00:27:54.480 --> 00:27:55.990
And the key here
is that we provide

00:27:55.990 --> 00:27:59.980
a comprehensive platform
for working with IoT data.

00:27:59.980 --> 00:28:02.950
And what's interesting
is, as we follow

00:28:02.950 --> 00:28:05.560
this data through the
journey from the Edge

00:28:05.560 --> 00:28:08.210
to the application,
it begins, perhaps,

00:28:08.210 --> 00:28:10.980
as highly specific
data that might

00:28:10.980 --> 00:28:14.480
be coming from a machine
specific industrial protocol

00:28:14.480 --> 00:28:16.230
or health care device.

00:28:16.230 --> 00:28:18.940
But as it moves and is
ingested into the platform,

00:28:18.940 --> 00:28:21.040
and processed, and
normalized, and standardized

00:28:21.040 --> 00:28:24.200
into the structured data that's
well suited for our big data

00:28:24.200 --> 00:28:28.950
tool chain, it begins to lose
some of it's IoT specificity.

00:28:28.950 --> 00:28:31.380
Temperatures just
become integers.

00:28:31.380 --> 00:28:35.270
And cycle times on machines
become just timestamps.

00:28:35.270 --> 00:28:37.820
And so as our general
purpose dictated

00:28:37.820 --> 00:28:42.220
tool chain processes and sort
of munches this IoT data,

00:28:42.220 --> 00:28:45.290
it then becomes a
bit more generic.

00:28:45.290 --> 00:28:47.500
And as it moves out
into the application

00:28:47.500 --> 00:28:50.370
is where really regains
this domain specificity.

00:28:50.370 --> 00:28:53.730
Because, at the end of the day,
the insights in and sort of

00:28:53.730 --> 00:28:56.030
queries that you
run against the data

00:28:56.030 --> 00:28:58.390
are what turn this
volume of data

00:28:58.390 --> 00:29:01.830
into the useful sort of
actionable level insights

00:29:01.830 --> 00:29:05.480
that you need to then present
back to processes or users

00:29:05.480 --> 00:29:07.930
through applications.

00:29:07.930 --> 00:29:10.800
And this is really why
application sort of

00:29:10.800 --> 00:29:12.470
called out here separately.

00:29:12.470 --> 00:29:15.490
And I want to tell you one
such application that we've

00:29:15.490 --> 00:29:17.580
incubated at Google
for Work, and launched

00:29:17.580 --> 00:29:20.430
in a multi-store pilot
with retail customer,

00:29:20.430 --> 00:29:24.300
is the solution which provides
digital inventory, visibility,

00:29:24.300 --> 00:29:25.700
and accuracy.

00:29:25.700 --> 00:29:30.630
This works by putting RFID tags
on every item in the store.

00:29:30.630 --> 00:29:33.680
Readers then on the
ceiling can scan the store

00:29:33.680 --> 00:29:36.710
and basically generate
a live inventory

00:29:36.710 --> 00:29:39.450
feed of what is both in
the front of the store

00:29:39.450 --> 00:29:41.490
and the back of the store.

00:29:41.490 --> 00:29:43.370
This application then
generates the sort

00:29:43.370 --> 00:29:46.530
of steady stream of the real
time view of the inventory

00:29:46.530 --> 00:29:49.690
up to Cloud Platform, where
it's processed and analyzed

00:29:49.690 --> 00:29:51.720
and then made available
as an application

00:29:51.720 --> 00:29:53.610
to store associates.

00:29:53.610 --> 00:29:56.280
Now store associates can
take this information

00:29:56.280 --> 00:29:58.710
and it boosts their
visibility into the accuracy

00:29:58.710 --> 00:30:03.020
of their inventory in-store
upwards of 95% compared

00:30:03.020 --> 00:30:05.300
to the 60% to 70%
typically gained

00:30:05.300 --> 00:30:08.250
through a standard
point of sale system.

00:30:08.250 --> 00:30:10.910
And this allows them to track
inventory in the back room

00:30:10.910 --> 00:30:12.670
to the front of
the store to make

00:30:12.670 --> 00:30:14.360
sure the product
is always present

00:30:14.360 --> 00:30:16.459
where customers need it.

00:30:16.459 --> 00:30:19.000
The corporate office, this also
gives all sorts of visibility

00:30:19.000 --> 00:30:22.570
about changing and adapting
marketing campaigns,

00:30:22.570 --> 00:30:25.520
adapting store HR
hours, et cetera.

00:30:25.520 --> 00:30:29.310
And once infrastructure
such as this is in place,

00:30:29.310 --> 00:30:31.720
it begins subsequent ideas
for other applications

00:30:31.720 --> 00:30:34.840
they can bring, sort of what
were once considered far

00:30:34.840 --> 00:30:38.560
off, future ideas sort
of virtual changing rooms

00:30:38.560 --> 00:30:42.750
and try on stores, touchless
payment checkout systems,

00:30:42.750 --> 00:30:43.250
et cetera.

00:30:46.020 --> 00:30:48.510
So this is going to bring
us back to security now.

00:30:48.510 --> 00:30:50.430
And security like
Device Management

00:30:50.430 --> 00:30:54.650
is something that needs to touch
the entire data handling stack

00:30:54.650 --> 00:30:56.300
end-to-end.

00:30:56.300 --> 00:30:58.670
And you all were in
the keynote earlier.

00:30:58.670 --> 00:31:01.640
Hopefully you've heard
how much attention we pay

00:31:01.640 --> 00:31:04.542
to security and Cloud Platform.

00:31:04.542 --> 00:31:06.500
It's something that Google
takes very seriously

00:31:06.500 --> 00:31:09.620
in Cloud Platform, builds
into the fabric of all

00:31:09.620 --> 00:31:11.760
of our products and services.

00:31:11.760 --> 00:31:15.470
Now for IoT, it's important
that we offer both secure APIs

00:31:15.470 --> 00:31:18.430
and methods for bringing
the data onto the platform

00:31:18.430 --> 00:31:22.164
securely, as well as sort
of the security controls

00:31:22.164 --> 00:31:23.830
and foundations to
make sure that you're

00:31:23.830 --> 00:31:29.430
able to keep that data secure,
once it's on the Cloud.

00:31:29.430 --> 00:31:32.660
On the device side, you've
heard about Brillo and Weave.

00:31:32.660 --> 00:31:34.870
Device security
needs to be built

00:31:34.870 --> 00:31:37.740
into the fundamental systems and
technologies you're choosing.

00:31:37.740 --> 00:31:41.880
So Sandboxing allows
the protection

00:31:41.880 --> 00:31:44.820
of one process from another.

00:31:44.820 --> 00:31:47.040
Over-the-air updates
and verified boot

00:31:47.040 --> 00:31:49.560
makes sure that you're
running secure code

00:31:49.560 --> 00:31:53.380
and always able to push out
the latest secure versions.

00:31:53.380 --> 00:31:56.260
Keeping user data secure on
the device and in transit

00:31:56.260 --> 00:31:58.970
to the Cloud is very important
for consumer applications,

00:31:58.970 --> 00:32:02.020
as well as commercial
applications.

00:32:02.020 --> 00:32:05.400
And finally, using
secure Linux technology,

00:32:05.400 --> 00:32:07.060
such as mandatory
access, controls

00:32:07.060 --> 00:32:11.010
limits the power of any
exploit from doing more damage

00:32:11.010 --> 00:32:14.390
in taking control of the device.

00:32:14.390 --> 00:32:17.660
But it's not enough for us to
just do what we can at Google.

00:32:17.660 --> 00:32:19.410
To really secure the
Internet of Things

00:32:19.410 --> 00:32:24.520
requires collaboration across
a broad ecosystem of partners.

00:32:24.520 --> 00:32:27.940
So one of the challenges
in IoT application

00:32:27.940 --> 00:32:31.340
is maintaining this end-to-end
integrity and secure ownership

00:32:31.340 --> 00:32:32.219
of the device.

00:32:32.219 --> 00:32:34.260
I want to tell you about
this collaboration we're

00:32:34.260 --> 00:32:36.640
working on with Intel in
the technology they're

00:32:36.640 --> 00:32:38.960
calling Marshal Point.

00:32:38.960 --> 00:32:42.230
The idea is that by
taking a hardware

00:32:42.230 --> 00:32:46.802
proof of authentication
in this technology

00:32:46.802 --> 00:32:49.010
that Intel calls EPIC, which
they've already licensed

00:32:49.010 --> 00:32:54.230
to Atmel and Microchip, that
a new owner of a device,

00:32:54.230 --> 00:32:57.740
a new service of a device, can
meet at a virtual rendezvous

00:32:57.740 --> 00:33:02.050
point, the manufacturer, the
previous owner of that device,

00:33:02.050 --> 00:33:05.290
and visually sort through
some cryptographic signatures,

00:33:05.290 --> 00:33:07.150
take new ownership
of that device

00:33:07.150 --> 00:33:10.120
and securely provision it
with application or Cloud

00:33:10.120 --> 00:33:11.776
credentials.

00:33:11.776 --> 00:33:13.150
So this is an
example but I think

00:33:13.150 --> 00:33:17.350
it takes to really secure
the IoT end-to-end.

00:33:17.350 --> 00:33:20.470
Because if you have
any gap in that chain,

00:33:20.470 --> 00:33:24.630
that becomes sort of an
unacceptable point of surface

00:33:24.630 --> 00:33:27.630
to breach your system overall.

00:33:27.630 --> 00:33:31.620
So in conclusion,
why do you want

00:33:31.620 --> 00:33:33.120
to get started building IoT now?

00:33:33.120 --> 00:33:36.680
Well it is a fantastic
time with the hardware.

00:33:36.680 --> 00:33:38.820
The proliferation
in the diversity

00:33:38.820 --> 00:33:41.250
of sensors, and
platforms, and hardware,

00:33:41.250 --> 00:33:44.720
it's never been easier to get
started with hardware today.

00:33:44.720 --> 00:33:47.780
Second, Google is bringing
it expertise and experience

00:33:47.780 --> 00:33:52.120
working with both devices and
data across the entire spectrum

00:33:52.120 --> 00:33:54.630
of IT problem space.

00:33:54.630 --> 00:33:58.460
And third, we do so within
the utmost focus on security

00:33:58.460 --> 00:34:00.460
throughout, as
you've been hearing.

00:34:00.460 --> 00:34:04.280
So I encourage you all to think
about the analog information

00:34:04.280 --> 00:34:06.870
that's relevant to you it
out there in the world today.

00:34:06.870 --> 00:34:08.340
And to go start
building solutions

00:34:08.340 --> 00:34:11.087
to capture and process
that information on Cloud.

00:34:11.087 --> 00:34:11.920
Thank you very much.

00:34:11.920 --> 00:34:13.469
We'll let you get
cut for your lunch.

00:34:13.469 --> 00:34:15.885
If anyone has questions, Jeff
and I will be down up front.

00:34:15.885 --> 00:34:18.279
[APPLAUSE]

